.TH LOGIND 8
.SH NAME
logind \- login daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/logind
[
.BI -g " group"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Logind
is normally started by
.IR svc (8)
to service requests on the
.B inflogin
TCP/IP port,
to provide a `signing' service (identity authentication) for a network.
Ultimately, the client receives a certificate that can be used to establish its identity
with any host that is willing to honour certificates from the certificate's signer.
.PP
The signer constructs the certificate from the contents
of the signer's key file
.BR /keydb/signerkey ,
typically created by
.IR createsignerkey (8),
and
the ID string and password supplied by the client (which are used to access
.IR keyfs (4)
to check identity).
.PP
The protocol involves an exchange of information between the client and server,
as summarised in
.IR login (6).
The client side of this exchange can be managed by the
.B Login
module; see
.IR security-login (2).
.PP
Client and server communicate over the Secure Socket Layer device
.IR ssl (3).
.PP
The exchange uses one of the three Diffie-Hellman groups
.IR login (6)
names, not the parameters in the signer's key file:
RFC 3526's group 14 (2048 bits), or group 15 (3072 bits) when the client
asks for the CNSA suite.
The
.B -g
option names the least group to offer (14, 15 or 16) if a site wants a larger one;
a client takes any of the three that is large enough for its mode.
The
.I alpha
and
.I p
of the key file are sent to the client once it has proved the password,
for
.IR security-auth (2)
to use between the holders of the signer's certificates.
In CNSA mode
.RB ( /env/cnsamode
set)
.I logind
refuses a client that does not offer the CNSA suite, saying so.
.PP
The name a client gives must be one account name, as
.IR login (6)
defines it (no
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
not
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
no control character);
any other is refused with
.B "bad user name"
before it is acknowledged, counted or looked up.
The signer's own name (the owner of
.BR /keydb/signerkey )
is not an account's, whatever
.IR keyfs (4)
has:
its certificate is the one in the key file, which
.IR keysrv (4)
shows to prove it is the signer.
.PP
A client of the exchange before version 2 is told
.BR "signer speaks login version 2" ;
nothing is negotiated.
.SS "Failed attempts"
.I Logind
limits online guessing of passwords
(NIST SP 800-53 AC-7, Common Criteria FIA_AFL.1).
After 10 logins in a row for a name that do not prove its password,
further logins for that name are refused for 60 seconds,
before the password is looked up or any key is exchanged,
with the error
.IP
.B "login refused: too many failed attempts; retry in"
.IR n s
.PP
An attempt is counted when it begins and its count cleared only when
the client's proof that it has the session's secret, which only the password gives,
has been checked;
.I logind
sends nothing made from that secret before then.
So a wrong password, a client that hangs up part way, a Diffie-Hellman value refused
(0, 1,
.IR p \-1
and the like, with which the secret would be known to all),
a time out and a
.I logind
killed are all counted, and many connections opened at once for one
name are counted as they start.
.IR Keysrv (4)
counts wrong proofs of a password in the same file.
A login that works clears the name's count.
A name with no account is counted and refused in the same way and the
same words, so the throttle does not tell which names have accounts.
Nor does a single login:
a name with no account, or one whose entry
.IR keyfs (4)
will not give (disabled or expired) or that has no usable secret or
expiry, is answered as a wrong password is.
The exchange goes on under a random stand-in for the password's SHA-256,
with the same work and the same messages, so the client's proof fails
and it is told
.BR "name or secret incorrect" ;
the real reason is written to the console alone, and the login is
counted and never cleared or signed.
A count not added to for 15 minutes is forgotten, as is a cooldown once
it ends; at most 4096 counts not at the limit are kept, the oldest
dropped first.
Each lock, and each refusal while locked, is written to the console and,
when the audit log is set up
.RI ( auditfs (4)),
to it as an
.B authlock
event.
.SS "Audit records"
When the audit log is set up,
.I logind
also records each certificate it issues and each attempt that fails, the name as one value
.RB ( Audit->value ):
.IP
.EX
logind authok user=\fIname\fP exp=\fIexpiry\fP
logind authfail user=\fIname\fP reason=\fIreason\fP
.EE
.PP
.I Reason
is
.B secret
(the proof was not the password's),
.B badvalue
(a Diffie-Hellman value out of range),
.B exchange
(the client went away or sent something else),
.B unusable
(no account, or one that cannot log in; the client is answered as for a wrong password),
or
.B badname
(not an account name; no
.B user
is recorded).
What a client is told never says more than
.BR "name or secret incorrect" :
when the key file or the count of attempts cannot be used it is told
.B "login service unavailable"
and the reason is on the console.
.PP
Each connection is served by a
.I logind
of its own, so the counts are kept in
.BR /keydb/loginfail ,
one line per name: the SHA-256 of the name in hexadecimal, the count,
when the last attempt began and when the cooldown ends (0 if not set),
in seconds since the epoch.
It is read and written whole under
.BR /keydb/loginfail.lock ,
made exclusively and removed when closed; a lock more than a minute old
is taken to be left by a crash and removed.
Removing an entry, or the file, ends a cooldown at once.
If the file or its lock cannot be had,
.I logind
refuses the login rather than go without the limit.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/loginfail.lock
.TP
.B /keydb/keys
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
.TP
.B /keydb/loginfail
Failed-attempt counts.
.TP
.B /keydb/loginfail.lock
Its lock.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/logind.b
